maria: Heads up — running into a weird hydration/import error when bundling the TS SDK into a Next.js edge function. Works in dev, fails in prod with `TypeError: require is not defined` during SSR. Anyone seen this?

devon: yep, classic ESM/CJS mismatch. Are you pulling the `dist/cjs` entry or `module`/`exports` field? Next edge expects ESM.

maria: I'm importing `@redwood/inference` (local alias) via `import { Redwood } from '@redwood/inference'` — package.json has both `main` and `module`. Next build seems to prefer CJS in prod though.

samir: check your bundler config — Vercel/Next will sometimes bundle `main` if the package exposes both. We had to add \"exports\" entry to force ESM for the edge target. Also watch for `type: \"module\"` in subpackages.

maria: ok will try `exports` override. also seeing auth failing in the edge: it can't access `process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY` at runtime (404 -> auth). is that expected?

devon: on Vercel edge, env vars are available but you must use `NEXT_PUBLIC_` for client-side. For edge functions they are at build-time unless set as runtime secrets. Are you using the private runtime keys?

maria: using build-time env in vercel.json: `env: { REDWOOD_API_KEY: "${REDWOOD_API_KEY}" }` — works locally. Production request shows token present in headers at build but then absent in the actual edge runtime request.

olivia: FYI we added a middleware in prod that strips `authorization` on certain routes for CSP reasons — could that be stomping the header?

maria: good catch, will check middleware. Also streaming responses seem to behave differently: on Edge I get a single chunk with EOF immediately instead of progressive tokens. Any streaming quirks?

samir: Edge runtimes use WHATWG streams (`Response` body) not Node streams. Make sure SDK uses `body.getReader()` path in the edge. We have a helper `readableToAsyncIterator` that branches on `const reader = body.getReader ? body.getReader() : nodeStream;`

devon: note: when bundling with Vite, some polyfills convert `globalThis.fetch` into a client shim that buffers bodies — that'll break streaming. Add `define: { globalThis: 'globalThis' }` or exclude the SDK from polyfills.

maria: code snippet of the failing import in case it's helpful:
```js
// pages/api/chat/edge.ts
import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference';
export const config = { runtime: 'edge' }
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
export default async function handler(req) {
  const stream = await client.chat.stream({ model: 'gpt-edge', messages: [...] });
  return new Response(stream, { headers: { 'content-type': 'text/event-stream' } });
}
```

olivia: for the `require is not defined` error, try forcing the ESM export in package.json like:
```json
"exports": {
  "./package.json": "./package.json",
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
    "default": "./dist/cjs/index.js"
  }
}
```

samir: also check the deployed artifact on Vercel — sometimes their builder hoists a CJS file into lambda. Look at the built `.next/server/chunks` files to confirm.

maria: thx. last thing — retry behavior: when edge function retries on 401 we sometimes see duplicated partial streams. Is the SDK idempotent-safe for streaming responses?

devon: not by default. We treat streaming as non-idempotent. For retries you should set `retry: { retries: 0 }` in the edge to avoid double streams, or implement `Idempotency-Key` and persist partial state. We're adding a flag `streamingIdempotent: true` in the SDK roadmap.

